        <span class="hero-eyebrow">Methods · Push Piers</span>
        <h1>Push Piers —<br /><em>What the Reaction Force Actually Means</em></h1>
        <p class="page-hero-sub">Also called resistance piers. What they are, how they use your own house to install themselves, and why that's exactly why they're wrong for most New Mexico homes.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="article-outer">
      <article class="article-body">
        <p class="article-lede">Push piers get recommended more often than New Mexico wood-frame construction can actually support. Understanding why comes down to one mechanical fact: the pier doesn't push itself into the ground. Your house does.</p>

        <div class="article-section" id="what-they-are">
          <h2>What a Push Pier <em>Actually Is</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">A push pier — also called a resistance pier — is a steel pipe section advanced into the ground from a bracket mounted at an existing footing.</p>
          <p>The name describes the installation itself: the existing structure supplies the reaction that presses the pier downward, section by section, as they're joined together. That's a fundamentally different mechanism from a helical pier, which is rotated in under torque, or a micropile, which is drilled and grouted. A push pier system is three parts — steel pipe sections, a foundation bracket at the footing, and a hydraulic ram using the building's own weight as reaction.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="how-they-work">
          <h2>How They Actually <em>Carry Load</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Once installed, a push pier is meant to carry part of the foundation load through the bracket and steel section down to a deeper bearing layer, or to a depth where enough resistance has developed.</p>
          <p>Installation pressure is useful field information while it's happening, but it isn't by itself proof of long-term capacity — the footing, the bracket, pier alignment, the soil profile, groundwater, and the structural load path all factor in too. It's also worth being precise about the vocabulary: pressure readings on a push pier are not the same thing as torque on a helical pier, and shouldn't be treated as interchangeable data.</p>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Worth knowing</span>
            <p>Push piers develop most of their capacity in compression, through end bearing and shaft friction. Resisting tension or uplift isn't typical of standard hardware — it requires a specifically engineered bracket or coupler detail that most installations don't include. If a job needs uplift resistance (wind, expansive-soil heave), that's a different conversation before piers are even proposed.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="good-better-best">
          <h2>Good, Better, <em>Best</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">The reaction-force requirement is the whole story here — everything about whether a push pier is the right call traces back to whether the structure above it is heavy enough to drive it properly.</p>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Good fit</span>
            <p>[TIM: to be filled in]</p>
          </div>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Better fit</span>
            <p>[TIM: to be filled in]</p>
          </div>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Best fit</span>
            <p>[TIM: to be filled in]</p>
          </div>
          <p>What rules a push pier out regardless of tier: an active plumbing leak, poor drainage, irrigation, or expansive-soil moisture change that's still unaddressed; a lightly loaded interior slab rather than an actual foundation load path; or a project that needs uplift/tension resistance without an engineered connection built for it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="installation">
          <h2>What Installation <em>Actually Involves</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Installation happens at the footing — excavation to expose it, the bracket mounted, then pipe sections hydraulically pressed and joined one at a time using the building's weight as reaction.</p>
          <p>[TIM: anything specific to New Mexico construction worth a homeowner knowing — how this plays out on slab-on-grade vs. pier-and-beam, older adobe or masonry footings that need extra care taking a bracket load, access realities in tight backyards?]</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="misconceptions">
          <h2>What People <em>Get Wrong</em></h2>
          <ul class="key-points">
            <li><strong>"A pressure reading is the same as helical torque"</strong> — they measure different things on different systems. Don't let the numbers get treated as interchangeable.</li>
            <li><strong>"Any house can take push piers"</strong> — the structure has to be heavy enough to supply real installation reaction. Most New Mexico wood-frame residential construction isn't.</li>
            <li><strong>"Pressed concrete piles are the same as steel push piers"</strong> — they're a related system (stacked precast concrete sections, also pressed using the structure as reaction), but not automatically equivalent to an engineered steel resistance-pier system. Ask about documentation, joints, alignment, and the bracket connection before accepting "same thing" as an answer.</li>
            <li><strong>"Push piers fix uplift too"</strong> — standard hardware is compression-only. Tension or uplift resistance needs a specifically engineered bracket or coupler most installations don't include.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>

